  <section class="workspace-card bunker-card" id="bunker-handover-context" aria-labelledby="bunker-handover-context-title">
    <h2 id="bunker-handover-context-title">AWS deployment handover</h2>
    <dl class="bunker-config">
      <dt>What</dt><dd>This EC2-hosted Nostr signing service: encrypted vault, AWS Secrets Manager unlock integration, approved clients and relays, and operating and recovery procedures.</dd>
      <dt>From</dt><dd>The implementation provider — responsible for delivering and configuring the service.</dd>
      <dt>To</dt><dd>The service owner's designated operations team — responsible for ongoing operation, access management, monitoring and recovery.</dd>
    </dl>
    <p><strong>Trust boundary:</strong> this is an online signer. The key is used on the server; security depends on AWS account permissions, host security and trusted operators.</p>
    <p><strong>Outside this handover:</strong> Bitcoin wallets, Lightning funds, hardware wallets, cold storage and proof of reserves.</p>
    <p><strong>Your task:</strong> verify the delivered service and record acceptance on behalf of the service owner. Their AWS administrator handles secret-version preparation and promotion; their node admin runs the checks and signs off.</p>
    <p>Handover is complete when the required checks pass and acceptance is recorded. Sign-off records operational acceptance; it does not transfer signing keys or change access permissions.</p>
    <p><a href="#bunker-identity-guide" data-bunker-go>Identify this deployment</a> · <a href="#bunker-acceptance-guide" data-bunker-go>Acceptance evidence</a></p>
  </section>
  <details class="bunker-workflow-help" id="bunker-background">
    <summary>What is a Nostr bunker?</summary>
    <p>Nostr is a protocol for signed messages called events, exchanged through relay servers. A bunker is a remote signer: approved applications request signatures while the private signing key stays with the signer.</p>
    <p>In this AWS deployment, an encrypted vault on the host holds the Nostr signing key. AWS Secrets Manager supplies its unlock value, and the running signer uses the key to sign. Remote signing keeps that key out of client applications; it does not isolate it from the signer host or remove trust in its administrators.</p>
    <p>Relays carry requests and responses. This page checks the deployed service, client policy and relay signing, rehearses AWS version promotion and recovery, and records the handover evidence. Passing these checks does not establish control of Bitcoin addresses or reserves.</p>
    <p>NIPs (Nostr Implementation Possibilities) describe the protocol:
      <a href="https://github.com/nostr-protocol/nips/blob/master/46.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NIP-46 · remote signing</a>,
      <a href="https://github.com/nostr-protocol/nips/blob/master/44.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NIP-44 · encrypted payloads</a>,
      <a href="https://github.com/nostr-protocol/nips/blob/master/01.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NIP-01 · events and relays</a>.</p>
  </details>
